From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 17:05:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: Add support for the A733 CPU CCU

The A733 has no driver for its CPU clocks. The two core clusters and the
DSU stay at the rates the boot loader set, and cpufreq has no clock to
change.

Add a driver for the CPU_PLL_CFG block. A cluster PLL and its EXT_DIVP
form one clock: N in the VCO range of 650 to 2260 MHz, P = M0 = M1 = 1
and EXT_DIVP /1, /2 or /4, the highest rate not above the request. The
manual's 288 MHz note on EXT_DIVP names a use, not a limit. Each mux
lists its parents in the order of the encoding (5.1.10.13, 5.1.10.25,
5.1.10.37), so every value the manual describes maps to its source; the
DSU's 100 is not described further and has no clock. The backup PLL is
only read. probe() refuses a slow clock other than the oscillator. A mux
that the boot loader left off its PLL is put back on it before the
clocks are registered, since a rate request goes to the source the mux
is on and the notifiers return a mux to its PLL. The mux switches
glitch-free; probe() moves it only to a PLL that is enabled, locked and
has its output on, at no more than the rate the cluster runs at the
lowest voltage of its supply (1014 MHz for the core clusters, 780 MHz
for the DSU), and otherwise ends without clocks. P, M0 and M1 other
than 1 and a reserved EXT_DIVP are only reported.

A rate change parks the mux on the slow clock. The notifier sits on the
PLL, and set_rate() writes N and EXT_DIVP while the cores are parked:
gate and lock detector off, factors, detector on, one update, wait for
it to clear, three lock reads in a row with the first 3 us after the
update, settling time, gate on. That is the order for enabling these
PLLs and for the main CCU's PLLs (5.1.4.2, 4.1.4.1, 4.1.4.2); the list
for a factor change in 5.1.4.2 repeats the update and waits for the
lock bit to clear instead of to set.

If the update is not taken or the PLL does not lock, set_rate() reports
it, leaves the gate closed and returns -ETIMEDOUT. The clock framework
drops that error, and the OPP core sets the voltage of the new rate all
the same. So the notifier, otherwise ccu_mux_notifier_cb(), moves the
mux back to the PLL only while the PLL runs; else the cores stay on the
park source, which runs at any voltage, until the next rate change.

Not covered: faster park sources and the DSU's AXI divider.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted on two boards; both core clusters
changed rate over their operating points under cpufreq without a CPU CCU
error, and on one board, with the B cluster mux set to the backup PLL in
U-Boot, probe put it back on its PLL. Not triggered: an UPDATE or lock
timeout, which leaves the gate closed and the cores parked.

Assisted-by: LLM
